l":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek","html_url":"https://github.com/dsnopek","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsnopek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"> Diving into the logic for `_convert_to_variant_type(...)`, we'd want to do the same for the `Variant::ARRAY` type as well, as multiple java types mapped to `Variant::ARRAY`.\r\n\r\nOh yeah, arrays of objects probably have the same issue. I'll make a test case for that, and then update the PR.\r\n\r\n> I also noticed we equate `String` and `CharSequence` as well. We may want instead to favor `CharSequence` by increasing its likelihood since a `String` is-a `CharSequence` but the reverse is not true.\r\n\r\nThat makes sense! Although, I feel like that's a sufficiently different issue, it perhaps should be a different PR?\r\n\r\nThere's some other issues I can see in there, like not ever giving `PACKED_INT64_ARRAY` or `PACKED_FLOAT64_ARRAY`, and instead using the 32 bit types. Trying to rationalize that whole function could be a follow-up.","created_at":"2025-05-28T18:02:43Z","updated_at":"2025-05-28T18:02:43Z","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/106908#discussion_r2112469037","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/106908","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/comments/2112469037"},"html":{"href":"https://github.co